From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 12:45:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] Make hardware rendering get a real framebuffer, every frame

A hardware-rendering client never gets its context reset in time, and on
Mupen64Plus-Next that is fatal rather than cosmetic: the core connects its
video plugin from inside context_reset (reinit_gfx_plugin ->
emu_step_initialize -> plugin_connect_all, which assigns gfx = gfx_gln64), and
its retro_run() switches to the emulator thread with no guard. main_run() then
reaches `if (!gfx.romOpen())` with the function table still zeroed and calls
through a null pointer.

The reset cannot be delivered while OpenStream() is running, because CStream
assigns m_handle from that call's *return value* - so GetHwFramebuffer() below
takes its !IsOpen() early return and answers 0, and libretro's GL state manager
caches that answer for the session in glsm_state_setup(). Kodi therefore holds
the request until the handle is live. But nothing asks for a buffer between
this Open() and the client running, so the request sat unclaimed and the client
ran uninitialised:

    RetroPlayer[RENDERING]: Render manager configured
    mupen64plus: [EmuThread] M64CMD_EXECUTE      <- 0.5ms later, same thread

Asking here is what claims it. Open() has returned, so m_handle is set and the
call reaches Kodi, which delivers the pending reset before answering - and this
is still OnFrameBegin(), ahead of the client's frame and therefore ahead of
romOpen(). The framebuffer is cached in m_framebuffer either way, so the frame
that follows uses it rather than fetching it twice.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/video/VideoStream.cpp | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

Two things stop it. Kodi's software and hardware streams disagree about
game_stream_buffer::type: GetBuffer() on the software side sets it as an output
and never inspects what came in, while the hardware side requires it as an
input and returns false unless it already reads GAME_STREAM_HW_FRAMEBUFFER.
This asked with a zero-initialised buffer, which is GAME_STREAM_UNKNOWN, so the
request was refused before it reached the stream and GetHwFramebuffer() could
only ever answer 0.

And nothing asked at all before the client's first frame. Kodi holds the
hardware context reset until the add-on's stream handle is live - it cannot be
delivered during OpenStream(), because CStream assigns m_handle from that
call's return value - and it hands it over on the first buffer request. With no
such request the reset stayed unclaimed and the client ran uninitialised. On
Mupen64Plus-Next that is fatal rather than cosmetic: the core connects its
video plugin from inside context_reset, and main_run() then reaches
`if (!gfx.romOpen())` with the function table still zeroed and calls through a
null pointer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/video/VideoStream.cpp | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/video/VideoStream.cpp b/src/video/VideoStream.cpp
--- a/src/video/VideoStream.cpp
+++ b/src/video/VideoStream.cpp
@@ -63,10 +63,30 @@
 
   if (!m_framebuffer)
   {
-    m_framebuffer.reset(new game_stream_buffer{});
+    // Filled in locally and only published once it holds a real answer.
+    //
+    // Requesting a buffer is re-entrant: Kodi delivers the pending hardware
+    // context reset from inside GetBuffer(), and the client asks for its
+    // framebuffer from inside that reset. Assigning m_framebuffer up front and
+    // filling it in place left that inner call looking at an allocated but
+    // still-zeroed buffer, so it was told 0 - and libretro's GL state manager
+    // asks exactly once, in glsm_state_setup(), and caches the answer for the
+    // session. The client then renders to the default framebuffer forever,
+    // which on a surfaceless context is nowhere.
+    std::unique_ptr<game_stream_buffer> buffer(new game_stream_buffer{});
+
+    // Kodi's two stream types disagree about what this field means. The
+    // software GetBuffer() sets it as an output and never inspects the input;
+    // the hardware one requires it as an input and refuses anything that is
+    // not already GAME_STREAM_HW_FRAMEBUFFER. Zeroed is GAME_STREAM_UNKNOWN.
+    buffer->type = GAME_STREAM_HW_FRAMEBUFFER;
 
-    if (!m_stream.GetBuffer(0, 0, *m_framebuffer))
+    if (!m_stream.GetBuffer(0, 0, *buffer))
       return 0;
+
+    // The inner call may have published one already; either is correct.
+    if (!m_framebuffer)
+      m_framebuffer = std::move(buffer);
   }
 
   return m_framebuffer->hw_framebuffer.framebuffer;
@@ -215,6 +235,23 @@
       m_streamType = GAME_STREAM_UNKNOWN;
     }
   }
+
+  // Take this frame's buffer up front, every frame.
+  //
+  // On the first frame this also claims the hardware context reset Kodi is
+  // holding. It cannot be delivered during Open(), because CStream assigns
+  // m_handle from that call's return value and refuses callbacks until it
+  // returns, so Kodi hands it over on the first buffer request instead. With
+  // nothing asking, the reset stayed unclaimed and the client ran
+  // uninitialised.
+  //
+  // Every frame after that is what keeps a picture on screen: OnFrameEnd()
+  // releases the buffer and drops it, and RenderHwFrame() submits nothing
+  // without one. Upstream relies on the client re-asking through
+  // get_current_framebuffer() each frame, which a client using libretro's GL
+  // state manager never does - it asks once and caches.
+  if (m_streamType == GAME_STREAM_HW_FRAMEBUFFER && m_stream.IsOpen())
+    GetHwFramebuffer();
 }
 
 void CVideoStream::OnFrameEnd()
